"field_subfield": "Mathematical Physics / Applied Analysis (nonlocal elasticity)",
"type_of_paper": "Theoretical / Mathematical Analysis",
"executive_summary": "This paper analyzes the Cauchy (initial value) problem for a general class of two-dimensional nonlinear nonlocal wave equations that model anti-plane shear motions in nonlocal elasticity. The nonlocal character is encoded through a convolution integral in the spatial variables, whose Fourier transform is nonnegative and satisfies a growth condition at infinity. The authors establish well-posedness, conditions for global existence, and conditions for finite-time blow-up of solutions for initial data in L2 Sobolev spaces. The central object is the equation w_tt = (β * ∂F/∂w_x)_x + (β * ∂F/∂w_y)_y, where F is a nonlinear function of |∇w|² = w_x² + w_y² with F(0) = 0, and β is an integrable kernel whose Fourier transform b_β(ξ) satisfies 0 ≤ b_β(ξ) ≤ C(1 + |ξ|²)^(-r/2) with r ≥ 2. The parameter r governs the smoothness of β and, as r increases, the regularizing effect of the nonlocal behavior increases. Local existence and uniqueness are proven by converting the problem into a system of Banach space-valued ordinary differential equations and using the operator K defined by the convolution and nonlinear terms. Global existence is established via conservation of energy, requiring a positivity condition F(u) ≥ -ku together with sufficient smoothness and the condition r > 4. Blow-up is proven using a concavity-type lemma, showing that solutions blow up in the L∞-norm of |∇w| when the initial energy E(0) is negative and a certain inequality on F'(u) holds. The paper notes that when β is taken as the Dirac measure, the model reduces to the quasilinear wave equation of conventional elasticity. The work extends prior one-dimensional nonlinear nonlocal Boussinesq-type results to two dimensions. The paper is purely theoretical and contains no numerical experiments, datasets, or computational simulations. It is supported by TUBITAK under project TBAG-110R002. The main contributions are the well-posedness theory, the global existence criterion, and the finite-time blow-up result, all grounded in functional analysis and energy methods.",
